extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Pie"
	- pagedescription = "Pie charts in Tablechart: parts of a whole as slices of a circle."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Pie
				p.lead Parts of a whole, as slices of a circle.

	+section("Pie chart")
		p Each row is a slice. The slices start at the top and go clockwise, in the order of the table. The names are shown in a legend under the pie.
		p A pie is a #[a(href=url('/types/donut.html')) donut] without a hole. It has the same colours, gaps and animation.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="pie")
				figcaption
					b Where the crop went
					br
					span thousands of apples
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Juiced', '38.2'], ['Eaten fresh', '18.1'], ['Sold whole', '12.4'], ['Composted', '5.8']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Numbers inside")
		p If a number fits inside its slice, it is shown there. If it does not fit, such as on a small slice or a narrow screen, it is next to the slice, with a short line.
		p The numbers inside all have one colour. It gets to be the one with enough contrast on the most slices, with the same settings as a number inside a bar. On a slice where that colour has too little contrast, the number is next to the slice. If you want all numbers next to their slices, add #[code data-chart-labels="outside"] to the figure.

	+section("A total row")
		p A pie has no middle, so it does not show a total. If a row has #[code data-kind="total"], it is not a slice. Screen readers read it in the table. If there is no total row, Tablechart adds a hidden row with the sum, as on a #[a(href=url('/types/donut.html#the-number-in-the-middle')) donut].

	+section("Gaps and corners")
		p The gaps between the slices go from the middle to the edge. If you want wider or no gaps, set #[code --tablechart-slice-gap]. With #[code --tablechart-slice-radius], the outer corners of the slices are round.

		+style.
			.round-pie {
				--tablechart-slice-gap: 6;
				--tablechart-slice-radius: 6px;
			}

		+chart
			figure.round-pie(data-chart="pie")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '42'], ['Jonagold', '28'], ['Braeburn', '18'], ['Gala', '12']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("A sweep")
		p With #[code data-chart-animate="sweep"], the slices go round in one motion, at one speed. See #[a(href=url('/styling/animation.html#grow-or-sweep')) Grow or sweep].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="pie" data-chart-animate="sweep")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '42'], ['Jonagold', '28'], ['Braeburn', '18'], ['Gala', '12']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Next")
		p By default, all slices use one colour, each one a bit lighter. If you want to set a colour for each slice, see #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) Colours].
